A||||7||GT Bastard||Tiny bits of gravel/dirt on my pistons....||||1||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||05-19-2000||10:20 PM||||While I was pulling my front cylindar head, a few tiny bits of gravel/dirt fell into about 3 of my cylinders.  The problem is, the lodged themselves right up against the edge of the piston so I can't just pick them out. Is this going to really screw things up if I don't remove them?  I have no power in my garage(apartment), but I COULD go buy a portable vacuum and use that.. What do you guys think?  It's only a few tiny bits, but I don't know how big of a problem it's going to be.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cooter||05-19-2000||10:23 PM||||You could try some of the computer duster spray air and see if they will blow out.||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||05-19-2000||10:32 PM||||Good idea Cooter!  I might just try that tomorrow.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Linx||05-19-2000||10:44 PM||||Manually turn the crankshaft so the pistons are going down...they might leave the debris on the cylinder wall and you can wipe em out with a cloth.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRA||05-19-2000||10:50 PM||||I would try finding a long enough extension cord and borrow a vac from someone. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Linx||05-20-2000||02:21 AM||||nah- what's the big deal...?  you're gonna yank the block and have it rebored, anyways, right?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phaeton||05-20-2000||11:58 AM||||The gravel shouldn't be a problem, probably blow right out the exhaust.  The dirt is going to embed into the aluminum on the piston above the rings and spot polish the bore with every stroke.  Dirt on a small portion shouldn't do permanent damage as the rings spin while the engine runs and will dilute the damage done to them.<P>Put the piston to the top and use WD40 or equivalent to pressure float the dirt out of the hole.  Flatten the extension tube and get it right down to the ring land, be generous with the fluid and repeat the procedure a few times wiping with a clean cloth between sprayings.  Take your time, the heads are already off and no extra damage has happens to the block yet.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||05-20-2000||05:20 PM||||DO NOT MOVE THE CRANK if the dirt is wedging between the piston and wall. In one cylinder that would work. In multiple cyls the pistons wont all move down. 1 or 2 will come up. If the piston moves up and this is hard dirt then you'll score the walls.<P>try using a peice of copper wire to catch the dirt and knock it loose so the air will blow it out.||||reg||1
